window.__LDNDRC_PAGES = Object.assign(window.__LDNDRC_PAGES || {}, {"auth-csrf":"<p>The platform uses two credentials for two different jobs, and most of the confusing bugs in its history came from mixing them up.</p>\n<h2 id=\"credential-one-the-jwt\">Credential one: the JWT</h2><p><code>POST /api/auth/login</code> returns a signed token with a 24-hour TTL. It is sent as <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> on API calls. It is stateless: the panel verifies the signature and does not look anything up.</p>\n<p>This is the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"operator\" tabindex=\"0\">operator</span> and student <em>API</em> credential. It never reaches the browser as a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cookie\" tabindex=\"0\">cookie</span>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"credential-two-the-session-cookie\">Credential two: the session cookie</h2><p>The same login response also sets a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cookie\" tabindex=\"0\">cookie</span> scoped to <code>COOKIE_DOMAIN=.ros-platform.local</code>. That <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cookie\" tabindex=\"0\">cookie</span> is what makes one login cover four hostnames. It is what the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gateway\" tabindex=\"0\">gateway</span> reads when a student opens <code>editor.ros-platform.local</code>.</p>\n<p>The <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cookie\" tabindex=\"0\">cookie</span> is not the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"jwt\" tabindex=\"0\">JWT</span>. It is a separate, signed value whose only job is to identify the user to the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gateway\" tabindex=\"0\">gateway</span>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-csrf-stamp\">The CSRF stamp</h2><p>A <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cookie\" tabindex=\"0\">cookie</span>-authenticated request is vulnerable to cross-site forgery: another site can make the browser send the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cookie\" tabindex=\"0\">cookie</span>. The defence is a third value, <code>csrf_token</code>, returned at login and held in memory by the SPA.</p>\n<p>Every <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cookie\" tabindex=\"0\">cookie</span>-authenticated <strong>mutation</strong> must carry it:</p>\n<div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"text\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">text</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">X-CSRF-Token: &lt;csrf_token&gt;</code></pre></div><p>Reads do not need it. Creation, stopping, approval, and denial do.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-bug-worth-remembering\">The bug worth remembering</h2><p>The <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"csrf\" tabindex=\"0\">CSRF</span> check was applied too broadly and caught <code>/api/auth/login</code> itself. A returning visitor with an old <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cookie\" tabindex=\"0\">cookie</span> got <code>403</code> instead of a new login — locked out by the security feature meant to protect them.</p>\n<p>The fix was to exempt login from the check while keeping it on every mutation. The roadmap keeps this visible as R3&#39;s &quot;one small fix left&quot; until the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"deployment\" tabindex=\"0\">deployment</span> was verified live.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-the-join-endpoint-is-different\">Why the join endpoint is different</h2><p><code>POST /api/nodes/register</code> is intentionally unauthenticated: a stranger&#39;s laptop has no credential yet. It is protected by rate limiting and an optional pre-shared <code>JOIN_KEY</code>, not by <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"csrf\" tabindex=\"0\">CSRF</span>. See <a href=\"#/nodes-api\" class=\"topic-link\">Register, approve, poll</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\"># no token → expect 401</span>\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%{http_code}\\n&#x27;</span> -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8082/api/sessions\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># with token → expect 202 (or a session-already-exists error)</span>\ncurl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8082/api/sessions -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization: Bearer <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$TOKEN</span>&quot;</span></code></pre></div>","session-lifecycle":"<p>A <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span> is a student&#39;s private robot workspace. Asking for one is fast; getting one is asynchronous. The API is honest about that, which is the single most important detail to internalise.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-five-calls\">The five calls</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">BASE=http://127.0.0.1:8082\nTOKEN=$(curl -s -X POST <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$BASE</span>/api/auth/login \\\n  -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Content-Type: application/json&#x27;</span> \\\n  -d <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;username&quot;:&quot;alice&quot;,&quot;password&quot;:&quot;secret123&quot;}&#x27;</span> \\\n  | python3 -c <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)[&#x27;token&#x27;])&quot;</span>)\n\ncurl -s -X POST <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$BASE</span>/api/sessions -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization: Bearer <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$TOKEN</span>&quot;</span>   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 202 provisioning</span>\ncurl -s <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$BASE</span>/api/sessions -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization: Bearer <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$TOKEN</span>&quot;</span>           <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># list, poll status</span>\ncurl -s <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$BASE</span>/api/sessions/&lt;<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">id</span>&gt; -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization: Bearer <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$TOKEN</span>&quot;</span>      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># detail</span>\ncurl -s -X DELETE <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$BASE</span>/api/sessions/&lt;<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">id</span>&gt; -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization: Bearer <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$TOKEN</span>&quot;</span></code></pre></div><h2 id=\"why-202-and-not-200\">Why 202 and not 200</h2><p><code>POST /api/sessions</code> has done its job when the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"deployment\" tabindex=\"0\">Deployment</span> object exists. It has not done its job when a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> is running. Returning <code>200</code> would be a lie, so it returns <code>202 Accepted</code> with <code>status: provisioning</code>, and the client polls.</p>\n<p>The <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gateway\" tabindex=\"0\">gateway</span> enforces the same honesty: it answers <code>503</code> until the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"deployment\" tabindex=\"0\">Deployment</span> reports an available replica. A student cannot reach an editor that is not ready.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-status-ladder\">The status ladder</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"text\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">text</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">provisioning ──▶ ready ──▶ stopping ──▶ stopped\n      │\n      └──▶ error</code></pre></div><p>Every state has exactly one sensible next action, which is why the UI can be simple: launch, wait, open, stop.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-ready-actually-means\">What &quot;ready&quot; actually means</h2><p>Not &quot;the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> is Running&quot;. It means the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"deployment\" tabindex=\"0\">Deployment</span> has at least one available replica, which means the readiness probe passed. A <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> can be <code>Running</code> and still be useless because <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"code-server\" tabindex=\"0\">code-server</span> has not bound its port yet. This distinction is the difference between a launch screen that lies and one that does not.</p>\n<h2 id=\"teardown\">Teardown</h2><p><code>DELETE</code> removes the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"deployment\" tabindex=\"0\">Deployment</span>, the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"service\" tabindex=\"0\">Service</span>, and the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pvc\" tabindex=\"0\">PVC</span>. That is the whole <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span>, including the student&#39;s home directory. It is deliberately not soft-deleted; see <a href=\"#/durable-state\" class=\"topic-link\">Surviving a redeploy</a> for the storage caveats.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">curl -s <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$BASE</span>/api/sessions -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization: Bearer <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$TOKEN</span>&quot;</span> | python3 -m json.tool\nkubectl -n ldndrc get pods -w</code></pre></div><p>Watch the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> appear while the API still says <code>provisioning</code>. That gap is the design.</p>","per-user-workload":"<p>The unit of tenancy is not a database row. It is a set of <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"kubernetes\" tabindex=\"0\">Kubernetes</span> objects with the student&#39;s name on it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-panel-creates\">What the panel creates</h2><p>For each <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span>, in the <code>ldndrc</code> <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"namespace\" tabindex=\"0\">namespace</span>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Object</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Deployment</code></td>\n<td>Runs the workspace <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span>; carries the image, env, and resource requests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Service</code></td>\n<td>A stable in-cluster address the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gateway\" tabindex=\"0\">gateway</span> can proxy to</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>PersistentVolumeClaim</code></td>\n<td><code>5Gi</code> by default, mounted as the student&#39;s home directory</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>All three are named from the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span> ID, so teardown is a single lookup.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-a-deployment-and-not-a-bare-pod\">Why a Deployment and not a bare Pod</h2><p>A bare <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">Pod</span> is not restarted if the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">node</span> reboots. A <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"deployment\" tabindex=\"0\">Deployment</span> gives you a ReplicaSet, a rollout strategy, and a readiness signal the panel can read. The panel only needs one replica, but it needs the controller semantics around it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-the-service-account-is-namespace-scoped\">Why the service account is namespace-scoped</h2><p>The panel creates these objects through its ServiceAccount. That account can create and delete <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"deployment\" tabindex=\"0\">Deployments</span>, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"service\" tabindex=\"0\">Services</span>, and <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pvc\" tabindex=\"0\">PVCs</span> <strong>in <code>ldndrc</code> only</strong>. It cannot see other <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"namespace\" tabindex=\"0\">namespaces</span> and cannot list <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">nodes</span> cluster-wide.</p>\n<p>This is what keeps a compromised panel from owning the cluster. It is checked explicitly in the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"deployment\" tabindex=\"0\">deployment</span> checklist — see <a href=\"#/control-panel-deploy\" class=\"topic-link\">Deploying the control panel</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"resource-requests-are-the-scheduling-contract\">Resource requests are the scheduling contract</h2><p>The <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> requests CPU, memory, and <code>nvidia.com/gpu</code>. Those requests are how <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"kubernetes\" tabindex=\"0\">Kubernetes</span> decides which <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"host\" tabindex=\"0\">Host</span> can take it. When a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> sits <code>Pending</code>, the reason is almost always in one of two places:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>no <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">node</span> labelled <code>role=host</code> (see <a href=\"#/hardware-gate\" class=\"topic-link\">The hardware gate</a>)</li>\n<li>no allocatable GPU because <code>SESSION_GPU_LIMIT=1</code> on a GPU-less demo</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl -n ldndrc describe pod &lt;session-pod&gt;\nkubectl -n ldndrc get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp</code></pre></div><h2 id=\"the-volume-is-the-student-s-work\">The volume is the student&#39;s work</h2><p>Everything in <code>~/dev_ws</code> lives on that <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pvc\" tabindex=\"0\">PVC</span>. Tearing a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span> down with <code>DELETE</code> removes the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pvc\" tabindex=\"0\">PVC</span> along with the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"deployment\" tabindex=\"0\">Deployment</span> and <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"service\" tabindex=\"0\">Service</span>, so &quot;delete the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span>&quot; and &quot;delete my files&quot; are the same operation today. That is a known sharp edge, and it is part of <a href=\"#/durable-state\" class=\"topic-link\">Surviving a redeploy</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl -n ldndrc get deploy,svc,pvc\nkubectl -n ldndrc get pod -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,NODE:.spec.nodeName</code></pre></div>","ros-domain-id":"<p>If <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"namespace\" tabindex=\"0\">namespaces</span> already isolate <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"dds\" tabindex=\"0\">DDS</span>, why give each <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span> a different <code>ROS_DOMAIN_ID</code>? Because isolation you cannot see is isolation you cannot verify.</p>\n<h2 id=\"assigning-the-value\">Assigning the value</h2><p>The panel derives a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"domain-id\" tabindex=\"0\">domain ID</span> per <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span> and injects it into the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> environment. The value is unique across active <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">sessions</span> and stable for the life of the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span>, so a restarted container keeps talking to the same graph.</p>\n<p>Two more variables travel with it:</p>\n<div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"text\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">text</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">ROS_DOMAIN_ID=&lt;unique per session&gt;\nROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE=LOCALHOST\nRMW_IMPLEMENTATION=&lt;pinned&gt;</code></pre></div><p><code>LOCALHOST</code> is the important one. Even if two <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pods</span> somehow shared a network <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"namespace\" tabindex=\"0\">namespace</span> — a mistake in a manifest, a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"host\" tabindex=\"0\">host</span>-network patch, a debugging override — their <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"dds\" tabindex=\"0\">DDS</span> discovery would still not cross loopback.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-not-random\">Why not random?</h2><p>Random IDs collide. With a small integer space and a workshop of twenty laptops, collisions are likely enough to matter. The panel allocates and tracks them, so two live <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">sessions</span> never share a value.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-a-student-sees\">What a student sees</h2><p>Nothing. <code>turtlebot3-sim</code> starts <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gazebo\" tabindex=\"0\">Gazebo</span>, and its topics are visible to the student&#39;s own terminal. Peers on other laptops are invisible. The <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"domain-id\" tabindex=\"0\">domain ID</span> is infrastructure, not a setting.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-debugging-consequence\">The debugging consequence</h2><p>If a student says &quot;my <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">node</span> cannot see the topic&quot;, the answer is never &quot;another student stole it&quot;. It is one of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">node</span> was launched in a different shell without the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> environment</li>\n<li>the discovery range was overridden</li>\n<li><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gazebo\" tabindex=\"0\">Gazebo</span> is not running (see <a href=\"#/gazebo-gzweb\" class=\"topic-link\">Gazebo over a websocket</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That narrows a whole class of support questions to three causes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl -n ldndrc <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec</span> &lt;session-pod&gt; -- bash -c <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;echo $ROS_DOMAIN_ID&#x27;</span>\nkubectl -n ldndrc get pods -o jsonpath=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{&quot;\\t&quot;}{.spec.containers[0].env[?(@.name==&quot;ROS_DOMAIN_ID&quot;)].value}{&quot;\\n&quot;}{end}&#x27;</span></code></pre></div><p>Every row must have a different number.</p>","gateway-proxy":"<p>The <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gateway\" tabindex=\"0\">gateway</span> is the piece that makes three separate tools feel like one product. It is not a separate <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"service\" tabindex=\"0\">service</span> — it is the control panel&#39;s reverse proxy, and it routes on two facts at once.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-two-facts\">The two facts</h2><ol>\n<li><strong>Hostname</strong> → which tool is being asked for.</li>\n<li><strong><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cookie\" tabindex=\"0\">Cookie</span></strong> → whose <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span> it should be routed to.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"text\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">text</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">editor.ros-platform.local   + valid cookie → user&#x27;s pod :7682\ndesktop.ros-platform.local  + valid cookie → user&#x27;s pod :8080\ngazebo.ros-platform.local   + valid cookie → user&#x27;s pod :9002</code></pre></div><p>There is no path prefix and no query parameter. The hostname is the entire tool selector, which is why the four subdomains are not cosmetic.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-the-cookie-and-not-the-jwt\">Why the cookie and not the JWT</h2><p>Iframes and <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"websocket\" tabindex=\"0\">WebSockets</span> cannot attach an <code>Authorization</code> header reliably. A <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cookie\" tabindex=\"0\">cookie</span> is sent automatically by the browser on every request, including the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"websocket\" tabindex=\"0\">WebSocket</span> upgrade. That is the only reason the second credential exists — see <a href=\"#/auth-csrf\" class=\"topic-link\">JWT, cookies, and CSRF stamps</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-error-contract\">The error contract</h2><p>The <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gateway\" tabindex=\"0\">gateway</span> does not return vague failures. Each status means exactly one thing:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Status</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>401</code></td>\n<td>No valid <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"cookie\" tabindex=\"0\">cookie</span> — log in again</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>404</code></td>\n<td>No active <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span> for this user</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>503</code></td>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">Session</span> is still <code>provisioning</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>502</code></td>\n<td><span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">Session</span> <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> is unhealthy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>This is the single best debugging surface in the system. A student reading &quot;503&quot; knows to wait; an <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"operator\" tabindex=\"0\">operator</span> reading &quot;502&quot; knows to look at the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"websockets\">WebSockets</h2><p><code>/ws/{session}</code> proxies the upgrade into the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span>. Everything downstream — <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"code-server\" tabindex=\"0\">code-server</span>&#39;s terminal, <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gzweb\" tabindex=\"0\">gzweb</span>&#39;s scene stream, Selkies&#39; signalling — rides the same proxy. If one tool works and another does not, the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"gateway\" tabindex=\"0\">gateway</span> is usually fine and the upstream port is not.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">COOKIE=$(curl -s -D - -o /dev/null -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8082/api/auth/login \\\n  -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Content-Type: application/json&#x27;</span> \\\n  -d <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;username&quot;:&quot;bob&quot;,&quot;password&quot;:&quot;secret123&quot;}&#x27;</span> \\\n  | grep -i <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;^Set-Cookie:&#x27;</span> | <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">head</span> -1 | sed <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;s/^Set-Cookie: //; s/;.*//&#x27;</span>)\n\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%{http_code}\\n&#x27;</span> -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Host: editor.ros-platform.local&#x27;</span> -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Cookie: <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$COOKIE</span>&quot;</span> http://127.0.0.1:8082/   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 200</span>\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%{http_code}\\n&#x27;</span> -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Host: editor.ros-platform.local&#x27;</span> http://127.0.0.1:8082/                        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 401</span></code></pre></div>","durable-state":"<p>The panel keeps two small pieces of state: active <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">sessions</span> and <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"node\" tabindex=\"0\">node</span> approvals. Today both live on <code>emptyDir</code> — a temporary shelf wiped when the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> is replaced.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-already-works\">What already works</h2><p>Small restarts lose nothing. The panel writes <code>sessions.json</code>; on boot it re-reads it and adopts <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pods</span> that still exist. If a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span> is gone, the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">session</span> is marked <code>stopped</code> rather than pretending it is alive. That was CP21, and it removed a whole class of phantom <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">sessions</span>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-is-left\">What is left</h2><p>A full redeploy replaces the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pod\" tabindex=\"0\">pod</span>, and with it the shelf. The fix is a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pvc\" tabindex=\"0\">PVC</span> (or a small database) plus a rollout strategy that always keeps one panel answering:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>move <code>NODES_DB</code> and the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">sessions</span> store onto a <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"pvc\" tabindex=\"0\">PVC</span></li>\n<li>set <code>maxUnavailable: 1</code> so the old copy dies only after the new one is ready</li>\n<li>prove it by replacing the <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"deployment\" tabindex=\"0\">deployment</span> with live <span class=\"term\" data-term=\"session\" tabindex=\"0\">sessions</span> and asserting zero loss</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"why-it-matters\">Why it matters</h2><p>Every workshop begins with a deploy. Losing the state that says &quot;these laptops were approved&quot; means re-approving twenty machines by hand in front of a room. This is why R2-final sits at the top of the <a href=\"#/roadmap\" class=\"topic-link\">What is still left</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl -n ldndrc get deploy -o jsonpath=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.items[*].spec.strategy}&#x27;</span>\nkubectl -n ldndrc get pvc</code></pre></div>"});
